  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15759v1</id>
    <title>Securing AI Agents Against Prompt Injection Attacks</title>
    <updated>2025-11-19T10:00:54Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15759v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15759v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems have become widely used for enhancing large language model capabilities, but they introduce significant security vulnerabilities through prompt injection attacks. We present a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating prompt injection risks in RAG-enabled AI agents and propose a multi-layered defense framework. Our benchmark includes 847 adversarial test cases across five attack categories: direct injection, context manipulation, instruction override, data exfiltration, and cross-context contamination. We evaluate three defense mechanisms: content filtering with embedding-based anomaly detection, hierarchical system prompt guardrails, and multi-stage response verification, across seven state-of-the-art language models. Our combined framework reduces successful attack rates from 73.2% to 8.7% while maintaining 94.3% of baseline task performance. We release our benchmark dataset and defense implementation to support future research in AI agent security.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-11-19T10:00:54Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Badrinath Ramakrishnan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Akshaya Balaji</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21752v3</id>
    <title>Semantics as a Shield: Label Disguise Defense (LDD) against Prompt Injection in LLM Sentiment Classification</title>
    <updated>2026-10-13T03:55:48Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21752v3" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.21752v3" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Prompt injection can cause large language models (LLMs) to deviate from their intended tasks by following instructions embedded in untrusted input. We investigate whether changing a classifier's output labels improves accuracy under such attacks, using sentiment classification as a case study. Label Disguise Defense (LDD) replaces positive/negative with alternatives such as good/bad or cat/dog, introduced through demonstrations, and maps predictions back to sentiment classes. We analyze retained aggregate results for 200 IMDb reviews, nine recorded model configurations, and eight alternative label pairs under a fixed attack that always requests the incorrect class using the original labels. Averaged over four pairs per group, sentiment-associated labels outperform the other tested labels in all nine configurations, but exceed a baseline using the original labels and the same demonstration and attack conditions in only three. At least one alternative pair improves on this baseline in seven configurations, with the best pairs identified retrospectively. These results show that a consistent advantage between label groups does not imply consistent improvement over standard labels. Missing matched evaluations on unattacked reviews prevent separating changes in ordinary classification performance from changes in the effect of the attack.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-11-23T20:16:51Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>8 pages, 3 figures. Substantially revised presentation, analysis, and discussion</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yanxi Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ruocheng Shan</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2512.05745v1</id>
    <title>ARGUS: Defending Against Multimodal Indirect Prompt Injection via Steering Instruction-Following Behavior</title>
    <updated>2025-12-05T14:26:45Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.05745v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.05745v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) are increasingly vulnerable to multimodal Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI) attacks, which embed malicious instructions in images, videos, or audio to hijack model behavior. Existing defenses, designed primarily for text-only LLMs, are unsuitable for countering these multimodal threats, as they are easily bypassed, modality-dependent, or generalize poorly. Inspired by activation steering researches, we hypothesize that a robust, general defense independent of modality can be achieved by steering the model's behavior in the representation space. Through extensive experiments, we discover that the instruction-following behavior of MLLMs is encoded in a subspace. Steering along directions within this subspace can enforce adherence to user instructions, forming the basis of a defense. However, we also found that a naive defense direction could be coupled with a utility-degrading direction, and excessive intervention strength harms model performance. To address this, we propose ARGUS, which searches for an optimal defense direction within the safety subspace that decouples from the utility degradation direction, further combining adaptive strength steering to achieve a better safety-utility trade-off. ARGUS also introduces lightweight injection detection stage to activate the defense on-demand, and a post-filtering stage to verify defense success. Experimental results show that ARGUS can achieve robust defense against multimodal IPI while maximally preserving the MLLM's utility.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MM" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-12-05T14:26:45Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Weikai Lu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ziqian Zeng</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kehua Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Haoran Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Huiping Zhuang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ruidong Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Cen Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Peng</name>
    </author>
